-- The empty page built for the `browser` platform, whose markup runtime is
-- the beni module `Rt` beside a hand-written file that imports it
-- (boundary.md §9.2, *A runtime module*; backend.md §15.1): one file under
-- `--release`, in which the module's functions are compiled with the program
-- and only the ones the page reaches are written — `template`, `childHtml`
-- and what they call, the render loop, `run` and the mount — and nothing of
-- the hand-written half, which the empty page does not reach. `run(…)` and
-- `export{… as flush}` name the module's declarations. Each function is the
-- hand-written runtime's it replaced, in shape (research 47 §4.3), and the
-- page is specialised to what it passes (backend.md §9). `Browser.program`
-- is beni too, and the entry's `run(main)` is read as a call (§9, *The
-- entry's call is a call*), so `run` sees a mount with no `h`: it has no
-- hosted branch, and `flush` no after-render phase. Every mount is at the
-- body (its `n` is always `null`), every instance's ends are nodes, so
-- `first` and `last` are reads; `unit` writes an instance's `b` before
-- anything reads it, so `patch`'s `b === i.b` compares the one block with
-- itself and `patch` returns the instance it is given (§9, *definite
-- initialisation*); the body `run` hands `mount` is past its guard, so a
-- slot's `p` is never `null`, `parentOf` is the read and the slot's `m`
-- goes (*fact 5 past a guard*) — and what the page calls from one place is
-- written there: `slot` and `parentOf` in their callers, `drop` at the end
-- of `swap`, and `template`, called once at this module's top level, is its
-- cell beside the kind that clones it.
import Browser
import Html exposing (Html)


view : {} -> Html {}
view _ = <></>


main : Browser.Program
main = Browser.program { init = {}, update = λ_ m -> m, view = view }
